# Copy to server/.env for local development. Production platforms inject these
# as real environment variables and the file may be absent.

# Required. The GitHub account the whole site describes; every route reads from it.
# The server refuses to boot without it.
GITHUB_USERNAME=HiIts-Matt

# Optional, but omitting it costs two concrete things:
#   1. Rate limit drops from 5000 requests/hour (authenticated) to 60/hour per IP.
#      The catalog bounds its fan-out to survive that, but a few page reloads with
#      a cold cache can still exhaust it.
#   2. GET /api/github/contributions stops working entirely and returns 503.
#      The contribution calendar only exists in GitHub's GraphQL API, which
#      rejects unauthenticated requests outright.
#
# If PROJECT_REPOS lists only PUBLIC repositories, use a CLASSIC token with NO
# scopes ticked at all: https://github.com/settings/tokens/new
#
# Every call this server makes then reads public data — profile, public repo
# list, languages, READMEs, markdown rendering, and the contribution calendar.
# The token is purely a rate-limit key, so if it leaks it grants nothing a
# logged-out browser could not already fetch.
#
# If PROJECT_REPOS lists any PRIVATE repository, use a FINE-GRAINED token
# instead: https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens/new
#
#   Resource owner          your account
#   Repository access       Only select repositories -> the ones listed below
#   Repository permissions  Contents: Read-only  (Metadata: Read-only is added
#                           automatically and is required)
#
# Contents: Read-only covers everything this server reads from those repos —
# the `.portfolio` tree, project.json, article.md, raw media bytes, READMEs.
# Prefer this over the classic `repo` scope, which is all-or-nothing read AND
# write across every private repository you can reach, forever, sitting in a
# deployed environment variable so four directories can be read.
#
# Fine-grained tokens have worked with GitHub's GraphQL API since April 2023,
# so the contribution calendar is fine — but if /api/github/contributions
# starts returning errors under one, add Account permissions -> Profile:
# Read-only, which is the permission that call maps to.
#
# The calendar still reports private contributions (1032 of 1144 for this
# account) because "Include private contributions on my profile" is switched on
# in GitHub's settings, which publishes the totals. No scope is involved; do not
# add read:user for this.
#
# Naming which private repositories those contributions belong to is not
# possible at any scope: the contributions API anonymises them deliberately, and
# counting them would mean granting `repo`, which reads all private source. Not
# a trade worth one statistic.
#
# `gh auth token` also works locally, but reuses the CLI's session token, which
# carries full repo scope and rotates on re-auth. Not for anything deployed.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Comma-separated whitelist of repositories for the dedicated projects page, as
# `repo` (owned by GITHUB_USERNAME) or `owner/repo`. Empty means the page says
# there is nothing to show. List order is display order.
#
# Repositories that share one story can be grouped in brackets:
#
#   PROJECT_REPOS=HiItsMatt,[Raspberry Pi: pi-stack, pi-homelab, pc-remote],MattBoard
#
# A group renders as one card titled "Raspberry Pi" that opens into its members
# in place. Groups own no repository, so the card's cover, languages and date
# come from the members; a group whose members are all unreadable is dropped
# like any other unavailable project.
#
# This is deliberately separate from the repo carousel on the overview page:
# the carousel is every public repo, automatically; this is curated work.
#
# Each listed repository may describe itself with a `.portfolio` directory on
# its default branch. All of it is optional — a whitelisted repo with no
# `.portfolio` still appears, described by its GitHub description or README:
#
#   .portfolio/
#     project.json          metadata; see below
#     article.md            the project page's body; see below
#     media/                images and videos, shown in filename order
#       01-hero.png         (numeric prefixes sort as you would expect)
#       02-tour.mp4
#       shots/              one level of grouping folders is also read
#         03-detail.webp
#
# Recognised media: .png .jpg .jpeg .gif .webp .avif .svg .mp4 .webm .mov .ogv
# Anything else in the folder is ignored, up to 24 files and 40 MB each.
#
# article.md — optional, up to 256 KB. Clicking a card on the projects page
# opens that project's own page: a slideshow of its media, this article as the
# body, and its metadata in a sidebar. The markdown is fetched raw and rendered
# by this server, so:
#
#   - Embedded HTML is escaped, not interpreted. The page is a document.
#   - `![Tour](media/02-tour.mp4)` becomes a video player; images work the same
#     way. Paths are relative to `.portfolio/`, as in project.json.
#   - Only files under `.portfolio/media` can be embedded — the same allowlist
#     the asset proxy enforces. An unresolvable image falls back to its alt
#     text, and a link into the repository's source tree renders as plain text.
#   - A repo with no article.md still gets a page; the body is simply its
#     summary.
#
# project.json — every field optional:
#   {
#     "title": "MattBoard",
#     "tagline": "Realtime collaborative whiteboard",
#     "summary": "Longer paragraph shown under the title.",
#     "year": "2025",
#     "status": "Shipped",
#     "tags": ["react", "websockets"],
#     "links": [{ "label": "Live", "url": "https://example.com" }],
#     "cover": "media/01-hero.png",
#     "media": [
#       { "src": "media/02-tour.mp4", "poster": "media/02-poster.png",
#         "alt": "Product tour", "caption": "Two cursors, one board" }
#     ]
#   }
#
# Media paths are relative to `.portfolio/`. Listing "media" makes it
# authoritative for order and captions and hides files it does not name; omit
# it to publish the whole folder in filename order. "cover" is moved to the
# front, and the page leads with it.
#
# Assets are streamed through this server (/api/github/projects/<slug>/media/…)
# rather than linked on raw.githubusercontent.com, which is what makes private
# repositories work in a public browser without exposing the token. Only the
# files discovered under `.portfolio/media` are ever servable.
#
# PRIVATE repositories require a GITHUB_TOKEN that can read them; a scopeless
# classic token cannot, and each unreadable entry is skipped with a warning on
# startup. Grant this with a FINE-GRAINED token scoped to exactly the
# repositories listed here (Contents: Read-only) — see the GITHUB_TOKEN notes
# above. The classic `repo` scope also works and is a much worse trade: it
# reads and writes every private repository you can access.
PROJECT_REPOS=

# Comma-separated list of browser origins allowed to call the API.
# Defaults to the Vite dev server when unset.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173

# HTTP port for the API. Defaults to 3000.
PORT=3000
